 "notes": "Libretto by Lorenzo Da Ponte (1787). Words follow the Schirmer vocal score's spelling, punctuation and syllables, as sung; lines are repeated as the score repeats them, part by part (\"ceder deve\" and \"un fido amor\" as Ottavio and Anna each sing them; \"con Proserpina e Pluton\" twice and \"l'antichissima canzon\" three times in the closing trio). The print has \"tesoro!\" and \"caro!\" where the libretto has commas, \"Ed io vado all'osteria\" (Castel's text: in osteria), and Ottavio's \"m'adora\" against Anna's \"t'adora\". \"all'\" is sung on the same note as \"vado\" (vado‿all') and \"osteria\" starts on the next note, so they are two words here. IPA, word meanings and translations were drafted for this app (S62), and the IPA was checked against Castel. IPA follows sung Italian conventions: stressed syllable marked, elisions (two words on one note in the score: Ottavio's me‿un, languire‿ancor, deve‿un, fido‿amor, Lascia‿o, anno‿ancora, vado‿in, Masetto‿a and Zerlina‿a, casa‿andiamo, vado‿all', Proserpina‿e, the trio's tutti‿o) joined with ‿, open e/o as ɛ/ɔ. Translation literal, for meaning.",
